  "final_text": "All four steps are finished, and I read the dashboard and the rule back from Kibana afterwards to confirm them.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' covers the index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, and its time field is `timestamp`. The response code is a text field, `response`, which also has a `response.keyword` version. Other fields include `bytes`, `clientip`, `host`, `request`, `url`, `geo.*`, `machine.os` and `agent`. There is also an `@timestamp` field, but the data view uses `timestamp` as its time field.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-a24805a8 Web logs' with exactly two panels. The first, \"Requests over time\", is a line chart of request counts over `timestamp`. The second is a bar chart of request counts for the top 20 values of `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-a24805a8 5xx errors' as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, already enabled. It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes, using `timestamp` as the time field. It counts requests whose `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\". Because the response code is stored as text, that range compares the values as text rather than as numbers. For normal three-digit HTTP codes it gives the same result as 500\u2013599.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\". Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, runs every minute, and has status \"ok\".\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
